<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<cfsSimulation xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
               xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation ../../../../../share/xml/CFS-Simulation/CFS.xsd"
               xmlns="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation">

  <documentation>
    <title>Coulomb friction in 3D: sliding along a diagonal direction in the tangent plane</title>
    <authors><author>kroppert</author></authors>
    <date>2026-08-06</date>
    <keywords><keyword>contact</keyword></keywords>
    <references>none</references>
    <isVerified>no</isVerified>
    <description>
      The 3D counterpart of ContactFrictionSlip2d, and the only friction testcase that reaches
      the genuinely three-dimensional parts of the formulation. Two things are invisible in 2D,
      where the tangent plane is one-dimensional:

      1. The slip DIRECTION is a free unit vector rather than a sign. The interface is dragged
      along the diagonal, u_x = u_y = 1e-3, so the traction must come out along (1,1,0)/sqrt(2)
      -- a direction no 2D test can produce and no bug that mixes the tangential components
      survives. 2. The slip tangent's direction term (mu*p/||s||) * (P_T - e e^T) is identically
      zero in 2D. In 3D it is a real rank-1 contribution, and this is the only testcase that
      executes it.

      Model: the ContactPatchTest3d geometry, two HEXA27 cubes with a coincident 2x2 interface,
      lower body fixed at the bottom, 1e6 Pa on the top, the interface dragged diagonally. As in
      2D the drag is prescribed AT the interface, not on the top face, which would carry a
      moment and open part of the interface.

      Every point is four orders of magnitude outside the cone, so all four slip:

      totalContactForce = p_N * A = 4e6 N (equilibrium, exact) totalTangentialForce = mu * F_N =
      1.6e6 N (Coulomb, exact)

      numStickPoints must be 0. totalTangentialForce is the magnitude of the RESULTANT, so it
      equals mu*F_N only because every point slides the same way -- itself part of what is
      checked. The pointwise maxima are slightly off their nominal values because the diagonal
      drag perturbs the pressure distribution; only the resultants are protected by equilibrium.
    </description>
  </documentation>

  <fileFormats>
    <input>
      <hdf5 fileName="ContactFrictionSlip3d.h5" linearizeEntities="__none__"/>
    </input>
    <output><hdf5/></output>
    <materialData file="mat.xml" format="xml"/>
  </fileFormats>

  <domain geometryType="3d">
    <regionList>
      <region name="master_elem" material="Aluminium"/>
      <region name="slave_elem" material="Aluminium"/>
    </regionList>
    <surfRegionList>
      <surfRegion name="master_iface"/>
      <surfRegion name="slave_iface"/>
      <surfRegion name="top"/>
      <surfRegion name="bottom"/>
    </surfRegionList>
  </domain>

  <fePolynomialList>
    <Lagrange><gridOrder/></Lagrange>
  </fePolynomialList>

  <integrationSchemeList>
    <scheme>
      <method>Gauss</method>
      <order>4</order>
      <mode>absolute</mode>
    </scheme>
  </integrationSchemeList>

  <sequenceStep>
    <analysis><static/></analysis>

    <pdeList>
      <mechanic subType="3d">
        <regionList>
          <region name="master_elem"/>
          <region name="slave_elem"/>
        </regionList>

        <contactList>
          <contactPair name="cube_contact"
                       primarySurface="master_iface"
                       secondarySurface="slave_iface"
                       slidingType="small"
                       normalPenalty="100"
                       initialGapHandling="fromMesh"
                       integrationOrder="2">
            <friction type="coulomb" mu="0.4" tangentialPenalty="100"/>
          </contactPair>
        </contactList>

        <bcsAndLoads>
          <!-- lower body fully supported -->
          <fix name="bottom">
            <comp dof="x"/><comp dof="y"/><comp dof="z"/>
          </fix>
          <!-- Drag the interface diagonally. Both in-plane components are prescribed, so the
               slip direction is (1,1,0)/sqrt(2) and the traction must follow it. z is left
               free: the normal support is still the contact interface, which keeps
               totalContactForce an equilibrium statement. -->
          <displacement name="slave_iface">
            <comp dof="x" value="1e-3"/>
            <comp dof="y" value="1e-3"/>
          </displacement>
          <pressure name="top" value="1e6"/>
        </bcsAndLoads>

        <storeResults>
          <nodeResult type="mechDisplacement"><allRegions/></nodeResult>
        </storeResults>
      </mechanic>
    </pdeList>

    <linearSystems>
      <system>
        <solutionStrategy>
          <standard>
            <setup idbcHandling="penalty"/>
            <matrix storage="sparseNonSym" reordering="noReordering"/>
            <nonLinear method="newton" logging="no">
              <lineSearch type="none"/>
              <!-- Increment-based convergence, as in ContactFrictionSlip2d: the residual is
                   normalised by the LINEAR right-hand side only, while the friction force
                   and the penalty reaction to the prescribed sliding both sit in the
                   solution-dependent one. Pre-existing openCFS behaviour. -->
              <incStopCrit>1e-9</incStopCrit>
              <resStopCrit>1</resStopCrit>
              <maxNumIters>20</maxNumIters>
            </nonLinear>
          </standard>
        </solutionStrategy>
        <solverList><directLU/></solverList>
      </system>
    </linearSystems>

  </sequenceStep>
</cfsSimulation>
